            <ArticleTitle>Pharmacokinetics of a new fentanyl tape with a novel delivery system of transdermal matrix patches in patients with cancer pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>10-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1159/000356625</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">A novel delivery system for transdermal matrix patches of fentanyl has been developed. The new fentanyl tape consists of a backing layer, a drug-containing layer and a release liner that is originally made of rubber adhesive. This open-label prospective study investigated the pharmacokinetics and safety of the fentanyl tape in patients with cancer pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The tape was applied to the patient's chest for 72 h, and serum samples were collected from 0 to 72 h. Fentanyl concentrations were determined using liquid chromatography with mass spectrometry. Pharmacokinetic parameters were calculated, and adverse events were evaluated according to the Common Terminology Criteria for Adverse Events (version 3.0).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Since 1 patient treated with 2.1 mg fentanyl was found to represent unique pharmacokinetic parameters by Shapiro-Wilk test, only 15 of the 16 study patients were investigated. Maximum serum concentrations (Cmax) and area under the curve (AUC)0-72 at 2.1 and 4.2 mg were 0.26 and 0.35 ng/ml, and 11.58 and 15.95 ng·h/ml, respectively. The Cmax and AUC0-72 tended to increase with dosage from 2.1 to 4.2 mg. There were no grade 3 toxicities, and only grade 1 ALT and AST elevations were observed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The novel delivery system of fentanyl may be a very promising approach to manage cancer pain clinically.</AbstractText>
